    description: "Residual of INV-mulul (process exit is not a subprocess), listed there and filed here so it is not lost. INV-mulul removed rust std::process exit/abort and elixir System.halt from subprocess. Two same-family rows remain, each needing its own decision rather than a mechanical deletion:\n\n1. swift.yaml process_send: Swift [fatalError, preconditionFailure, assertionFailure], note \"Swift process termination/assertion functions\". process_send = \"send a message to another runtime-managed process or actor\" and derives an ipc taint SINK, so a value passed as the message is a flow into the ipc zone. None sends anything to another process: they trap THIS process. But fatalError(message) prints \"Fatal error: <message>\" to stderr (and precondition/assertion failures do in debug builds), so the right home may be `logging` (haskell's System.Exit.die precedent, INV-dozuj) rather than no row. Needs the swift stdlib behaviour read per function and per build configuration.\n2. erlang.yaml process_send row for module erlang includes `exit`. erlang:exit/2 sends an exit SIGNAL to another process (process_send is right); erlang:exit/1 raises in the calling process (no crossing). The catalogue matches on name, not arity, so both are rowed. `exit` is already in erlang's ambiguous_names. Decide whether arity can be distinguished at the call site, or disclose the over-match.\n\nNot checked: kotlin exitProcess, scala sys.exit, objc exit()/abort(), c stdlib exit/abort/_exit -- grep found no rows for them on 2026-10-03, i.e. they already follow the \"no boundary\" ruling."  # 5cd8
    fields: {}  # 5cd8
